```grf:context id="client-guide"
{
  "format": "grf.public-document/v1",
  "title": "README",
  "content": "GRF downstream starter and admission client\n\nStart with QUICKSTART.grf. grf_starter.py creates a project-agnostic workspace with a pinned local runner, real acceptance commands, durable sessions, and hashed completion evidence. Local mode works without a hosted service. The optional API mode described below requires separate enrollment.\n\nAn MIT-licensed Python client for requesting a decision from a separately operated\nGRF authority before running an explicitly selected local command.\n\nRelease candidate 0.3.0-rc1. The hosted service is not publicly activated by this\nrelease. This repository contains the public client and protocol examples only.\nThe hosted evaluator and its rules are a separate private implementation and are\nnot licensed or distributed by this repository. No server, administrative access,\nshared credential, private policy, or working service address is included.\n\nRequirements and use\n\nPython 3.9 or later, using only its standard library. No local setup server or SSH\ntunnel is required for the intended hosted deployment. A real deployment requires\nseparate service enrollment: verified API hostname, CA trust material, a unique\nclient certificate and its locally protected key, project, and policy version.\nThe example hostname is deliberately nonfunctional.\n\n1. Obtain enrollment through the service operator's verified channel. Keep your\nprivate key local; never put credentials in this repository or issue reports.\n2. Copy enrollment.example.json to a private enrollment.json, supplying your\nissued values. Certificate paths resolve relative to that configuration file.\n3. Review and adapt example.grf. Its command, working directory, scope and\nidentity must match an action approved for your project. The sample is not\nautomatically entitled. Use an absolute executable and working directory in\nreal integrations to avoid PATH/directory ambiguity.\n4. Check local syntax without connecting or executing:\n\ntext\npython grf_client.py example.grf --config enrollment.json --check\n\n\n5. Request admission and run the command only if admitted:\n\ntext\npython grf_client.py example.grf --config enrollment.json\n\n\nTLS verifies the service hostname and CA and presents your client certificate.\nThere is no insecure-TLS option, credential fallback, automatic command retry or\noffline authorization. Denial, invalid responses and network failure prevent the\nwrapper from launching the command. Once launched, a command can have side effects;\na timeout is not a rollback and does not guarantee descendant termination.\n\nWire protocol and privacy\n\nThe only application route is POST /v1/admission, JSON over mutually authenticated\nTLS. Requests contain phase (issue or consume), project, policy, and\naction: SHA-256 of the action JSON serialized with sorted keys, compact separators\nand ASCII escaping. Consumption additionally supplies the ephemeral grant.\nThe action covers argv, cwd, scope, and identity.\n\nSuccess issues a short-lived opaque grant and then consumes it for the same\nauthenticated principal and binding. A denial contains only {\"allowed\":false}.\nPrivate rules, policy catalogs, evaluation traces and source are not API outputs.\nCommand text, local output and checks are not sent by this client. Digests are not\nencryption: predictable actions may be guessable. The server can see connection\nmetadata, certificate identity, project/policy identifiers and action digests.\n\nThe authority is designed to retain only minimal entitlement/credential bindings;\npending grants and request budgets are transient memory. A production operator\nmust separately verify proxy, platform, crash and backup retention. This client\ndoes not certify another operator's logging practices.\n\nSecurity boundary\n\nThis is an explicit command wrapper, not an operating-system sandbox. A person who\ncontrols their PC or modifies this code can bypass local admission. That does not\ngive them the server's code or access to its private rules. Mandatory enforcement\nrequires an independently protected executor for the supported environment; that\nexecutor is not included here. TLS protects the decision in transit, not a hostile\ncustomer's own computer. API answers necessarily reveal some policy behavior.\n\nThe sample implementation is not proof of legal compliance, arbitrary command\ncontainment, full GRF-engine equivalence, or a production service SLA.\n\nUpdates and removal\n\nUse versioned releases from the verified publishing account; compare the release\nmanifest with the independently obtained release checksum. A checksum bundled\ninside an archive provides integrity checking, not publisher authentication.\nReplace the client files to update or restore a previous verified version.\nEnrollment remains separate. Remove the client to uninstall and ask the operator\nto revoke the enrollment if access is no longer needed. No background service is\ninstalled by this client.\n\nLicense\n\nMIT; see LICENSE. This license covers these distributed client files only."
}
```
